MEMORY

Work from one session is gone next session unless remembered. Memory = key +
body. Body is prose or code, one slot: here they are the same material.

- (memory/remember key body) store one. Prose body is something to know.
  Quoted form body is a recipe to run later. Quote it: arguments are
  evaluated, so unquoted form stores its result instead.
- (memory/recall query) retrieve by key or body text, strongest first. Query is
  words, not a pattern: every meaningful word must land in the one memory.
  Spelling forgiven, prefix counts. Found memories load into your context:
  recalling IS reading. No echo after it.
- (memories) every memory as key, score and trigger, strongest first.
- (memory/forget key) drop one for good.
- (memory/forget-all) drop every memory, only when the user asks for a clean slate.
- (memory/replay key) evaluate a memory whose body is a form, return result.
  Replaying a defun installs that function. Remembered recipe becomes running one.
- (memory/revise key body) correct a memory. See THE WINDOW.

Names are slashed: bare remember, recall, forget are English verbs and would
be read as code in an aside.

FIRING BY ITSELF

With :on a memory watches for an event and loads itself, unasked. Trigger is a
quoted form of kind and pattern. Eight kinds:

  call     a form about to be evaluated, at any depth, so it catches a call
           nested inside a setq or a let
  result   the printed value a top-level form returned
  error    an evaluation that raised, so a past mistake surfaces exactly as it
           is about to repeat
  step     every step
  prose    words in the prose around your forms
  user     words in what the user just said
  recall   another memory being retrieved
  start    the user's first message, once, with no pattern: written (start)

For call the pattern is A FORM, written like the call, matched structurally:

(call (load-mcp "acme")) only that server, not every load
(call (load-mcp)) any call to load-mcp, whatever its arguments
(call (load-mcp _)) _ matches one argument, whatever it is
(call (acme/.*)) a head is a regular expression, so one
                                   pattern hooks every tool on a server
(call (setq _ (acme/get _))) the shape nests as deeply as you write it

Pattern list matches a PREFIX of the call: fewer arguments looser, more
tighter. Every other kind matches text: pattern is a string regex, matched
case-insensitively anywhere:

(user "file.*") whatever the user says about file.md, file.pdf or any other one
(user "^deploy") only what the user opens with the word

Invalid regex is read as plain text, so a literal with a stray bracket still
matches.

Combine with all, any-of and not, in either sort of trigger:

(call (all (load-mcp) (not (load-mcp "acme"))))
(error (any-of "undefined" "void variable"))

Memory on a loader call surfaces what you know about a server the moment you
load it. Memory on an error surfaces the fix the moment that error returns.

Start memory rides the user's first message and stays for the whole
conversation: put role and goal there. Loads once: a revision is read only by
the next conversation.

Fired memory arrives as a REPL feedback line, to you alone. Not run, user does
not see it. To run, replay. To show the user, echo.

CHAINING

Retrieval is an event, so one memory can hook another and both arrive
together. Say so outright with :links, a list of keys. Also automatic:
memories firing in the same step get wired together.

STRENGTH AND FORGETTING

Every memory has a score. Recall strengthens it. Unused, it fades, half its
strength a week, and below a floor it is swept away. No hand tidying needed;
memory/forget exists for what is wrong.

THE WINDOW

Retrieving a memory opens it: until step end you can revise its body. Outside
that window revise errors, telling you to recall first. Fix a memory while
looking at it.

GOTCHAS

- Body must be prose or a readable form. Closure, promise or secret is a live
  value, prints in angle brackets, and remember rejects it. Store the quoted
  form that builds it, never the built thing.
- memory/recall output is already in front of you. No echo after.
- Firing runs nothing. Fired recipe? Replay it.
- Triggers watch code you write into the REPL. A form evaluated inside a
  replay or a function body is not a step and fires nothing.
- Memories outlive the interpreter; definitions die on reset. A recipe worth
  keeping belongs in a memory, not a defun.
- Memory with no :on never arrives by itself. It waits for a recall naming it.
  Unguessable key later means lost. Correction stored without a trigger is the
  commonest memory that never helps again.

RULES

- REMEMBER THE MOMENT A GUESS FAILS. Rejected call, wrong name, backwards
  argument order: worth keeping. Write it before moving on, while the right
  answer is on screen. That is your only chance.
- GIVE IT THE TRIGGER THAT WILL REPEAT, not a topic description. Hook what you
  will be doing when you next need it. Misnamed tool goes on the call that
  loads its server, so it arrives before you misname it again. Rejected
  mistake goes on that error. A memory you must remember to look for, you will
  not look for.
- NAME THE ARGUMENT THAT MADE IT TRUE. Facts about one server are not true of
  the next, so (call (load-mcp "acme")) is right where
(call (load-mcp)) would fire on every server and teach wrong things about
  most. Hook the whole call, not only the name.
- Remember the correction, not the transcript. "browser_navigate, not navigate"
  is worth keeping; the whole tool listing is not.
- Do not remember what the REPL can tell you: doc output, discovery results,
  anything recomputable. Memory is for what you found out the hard way.
- Recall before you guess. Unsure of a name the session has not told you?
  Cheapest possible recall.
